q("he-111", I, 1, RD, "Using the same graph, how much lower was the temperature of Q than that of P after 15 minutes?", "20 " + D, [W("10 " + D, "wrong-reading", "At 15 min, P is 60 and Q is 40."), W("30 " + D, "wrong-reading", "At 15 min, P is 60 and Q is 40."), W("40 " + D, "wrong-reading", "40 " + D + " is the reading of Q, not the difference.")], ["60 - 40 = 20."], CG)
q("he-112", I, 2, HE, "Which statements about conductors of heat are correct?\nA: Metals are good conductors.\nB: Air is a poor conductor.\nC: Wood is a good conductor.\nD: Plastics are poor conductors.", "A, B and D only", [W("A, B and C only", "wrong-combo", "C is wrong because wood is a poor conductor."), W("B, C and D only", "wrong-combo", "C is wrong because wood is a poor conductor."), W("A and C only", "wrong-combo", "C is wrong, and B and D are correct as well.")], ["Metals conduct heat well. Air, wood and plastics are poor conductors."], None)
q("he-113", I, 2, HE, "At room temperature, a metal spoon feels colder to the touch than a wooden spoon. Why?", "The metal conducts heat away from our hand faster", [W("The metal spoon is at a lower temperature than the wooden spoon", "wrong-idea", "Both are at room temperature."), W("The wooden spoon makes more heat than the metal spoon", "wrong-idea", "Wood does not make heat."), W("The metal spoon gives heat to our hand", "reversed", "Our hand is warmer, so heat moves from the hand to the spoon.")], ["Heat leaves the hand faster through metal, so the metal feels colder."], None)
q("he-114", I, 2, RD, "Hot water was put in three identical cups, each wrapped in a different material, for 20 minutes. The table shows the final temperature. Which material is the best insulator of heat?", "Wool", [W("Newspaper", "wrong-material", "The water in this cup was cooler than in the wool one."), W("Aluminium foil", "wrong-material", "The water in this cup cooled the most."), W("They are equally good", "wrong-idea", "The final temperatures are different.")], ["The water stayed hottest in the wool, so wool is the best insulator."], tb(["Wrapped in", "Final temperature (" + D + ")"], [["wool", "64"], ["newspaper", "55"], ["aluminium foil", "41"]], [130, 150], "All cups started at 85 " + D))
q("he-115", I, 1, HE, "A glass bottle is filled to the brim with cold water and closed tightly. It is left in the hot Sun. What may happen?", "The water expands and may crack the bottle", [W("The water contracts and the level drops", "reversed", "Heated water expands."), W("The water turns into ice", "wrong-idea", "The Sun gives heat. Ice forms only when water loses heat."), W("Nothing happens to the water", "wrong-idea", "The water gains heat and expands.")], ["Water gains heat and expands. With no room, it pushes on the bottle."], None)
q("he-116", I, 1, HE, "A sealed empty plastic bottle contains only air. It is put in a freezer for an hour. What will happen to the bottle?", "It will be slightly squashed because the air inside contracts", [W("It will swell up because the air inside expands", "reversed", "Air contracts when it loses heat."), W("It will stay exactly the same", "wrong-idea", "The cooled air takes up less space."), W("It will burst because the air turns into ice", "wrong-idea", "Air does not turn into ice in a freezer.")], ["Cooled air contracts, so the outside air pushes the bottle in."], None)
q("he-117", I, 1, HE, "A table tennis ball has a small dent. When it is put in hot water, the dent pops out. Why?", "The air inside the ball gains heat and expands", [W("The air inside the ball loses heat and contracts", "reversed", "Hot water gives heat to the air."), W("The hot water goes into the ball and pushes out the dent", "wrong-idea", "The ball is sealed, so no water enters."), W("The plastic of the ball melts and fills the dent", "wrong-idea", "The plastic does not melt in hot water.")], ["The warmed air expands and pushes the dent out."], None)
q("he-118", I, 1, HE, "Which row shows what happens to a solid, a liquid and a gas when each gains heat?", "Solid expands, liquid expands, gas expands", [W("Solid contracts, liquid expands, gas expands", "wrong-combo", "A solid also expands when heated."), W("Solid expands, liquid expands, gas contracts", "wrong-combo", "A gas also expands when heated."), W("Solid expands, liquid contracts, gas expands", "wrong-combo", "A liquid also expands when heated.")], ["Solids, liquids and gases all expand when they gain heat."], None)
q("he-119", I, 1, HE, "A dentist chooses a filling that expands and contracts by about the same amount as a tooth when hot or cold drinks are taken. Why is this important?", "So that the filling does not become loose or crack the tooth", [W("So that the filling is heavier than the tooth", "wrong-idea", "Mass is not the reason."), W("So that the filling gets hotter than the tooth", "wrong-idea", "That would not help."), W("So that the filling never gains heat", "wrong-idea", "The filling still gains and loses heat.")], ["Matching expansion keeps the filling snug in the tooth."], None)
q("he-120", I, 2, HE, "A metal bench and a wooden bench in a park have been in the Sun for hours and are both at the same temperature. The metal bench feels hotter to the touch. Why?", "Metal conducts heat to our hand faster than wood does", [W("Metal makes more heat than wood", "wrong-idea", "Neither material makes heat."), W("Wood is a better conductor than metal", "reversed", "Metal is the better conductor."), W("The metal bench is at a higher temperature", "wrong-idea", "They are at the same temperature.")], ["Heat moves from the metal into the hand faster."], None)
q("he-121", I, 2, RD, "Three identical cups with lids hold hot water at 90 " + D + ". They are made of different materials. The table shows the temperature of the water after 30 minutes. Which cup is the best insulator?", "Foam cup", [W("Glass cup", "wrong-cup", "The water in the glass cup was cooler than in the foam cup."), W("Steel cup", "wrong-cup", "The water in the steel cup cooled the most."), W("They are equally good", "wrong-idea", "The final temperatures are different.")], ["The foam cup kept the water hottest."], tb(["Cup", "Temperature after 30 min (" + D + ")"], [["foam", "68"], ["glass", "52"], ["steel", "40"]], [90, 190], "Three cups"))
q("he-122", I, 2, HE, "Hot soup is poured into a pot. A metal ladle is left inside the pot. After some time, the top end of the ladle is hot. How did the heat get there?", "It travelled up the ladle from the soup", [W("It came from the cold air above the pot", "wrong-idea", "Heat flows from hot to cold, not from cold air."), W("It was made by the metal itself", "wrong-idea", "Metal does not make heat."), W("The soup gave coldness to the ladle", "wrong-idea", "Cold is not something that is given. Heat flows.")], ["Heat moves from the hotter soup along the ladle."], None)
q("he-123", I, 2, HE, "Why are the handles of cooking tools often covered with rubber?", "Rubber is a poor conductor, so less heat reaches the hand", [W("Rubber is a good conductor, so it spreads the heat", "reversed", "Rubber is a poor conductor."), W("Rubber makes the tool heavier", "wrong-idea", "Weight is not related to heat."), W("Rubber gives off cold", "wrong-idea", "Rubber does not give off cold.")], ["A poor conductor slows the heat flowing to the hand."], None)
